1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:01,947
You're falling toward Jupiter.

2
00:00:01,947 --> 00:00:03,403
Nothing can stop you now.

3
00:00:03,403 --> 00:00:05,034
And here's the strange part.

4
00:00:05,034 --> 00:00:07,073
There is no ground waiting for you.

5
00:00:07,073 --> 00:00:11,442
You could fall for seventy thousand kilometers, and never land on anything.

6
00:00:12,142 --> 00:00:15,806
Jupiter is the biggest planet we have, eleven Earths wide.

7
00:00:15,806 --> 00:00:21,934
Today we jump in, and we ride all the way down, floor by floor, to find out what's really inside.

8
00:00:22,634 --> 00:00:23,507
And it's heavy.

9
00:00:23,507 --> 00:00:27,291
Jupiter holds three hundred and eighteen times the mass of Earth.

10
00:00:27,291 --> 00:00:31,308
That's more than twice as much as all the other planets put together.

11
00:00:31,308 --> 00:00:34,218
Everything about this fall is going to be extreme.

12
00:00:34,918 --> 00:00:36,270
Here's how this works.

13
00:00:36,270 --> 00:00:39,710
Jupiter has no surface, so scientists pick a zero point.

14
00:00:39,710 --> 00:00:44,379
It's the height where the air pressure is the same as at sea level on Earth.

15
00:00:44,379 --> 00:00:44,871
One bar.

16
00:00:44,871 --> 00:00:48,127
Everything we measure today is depth below that line.

17
00:00:48,127 --> 00:00:50,707
And on the way, we'll watch three numbers.

18
00:00:50,707 --> 00:00:54,332
How deep, how hard the air is pushing, and how hot it gets.

19
00:00:55,031 --> 00:00:56,786
Floor one is still in space.

20
00:00:56,786 --> 00:01:03,551
Let's say you step off near Europa, one of Jupiter's moons, six hundred and seventy thousand kilometers out.

21
00:01:03,551 --> 00:01:05,180
At first, you barely move.

22
00:01:05,180 --> 00:01:07,435
But Jupiter pulls harder and harder.

23
00:01:07,435 --> 00:01:13,762
Our calculation: after almost fifteen hours of falling, you're going fifty six kilometers per second.

24
00:01:14,462 --> 00:01:15,890
And the view is unreal.

25
00:01:15,890 --> 00:01:20,483
From Europa's distance, Jupiter already spans about twelve degrees of sky.

26
00:01:20,483 --> 00:01:23,834
Picture twenty three full Moons lined up side by side.

27
00:01:23,834 --> 00:01:30,910
As you fall, it grows until there's nothing in front of you but clouds, from one edge of your vision to the other.

28
00:01:31,610 --> 00:01:38,463
Fall in from even farther away, and you'd hit the top of the air at fifty nine and a half kilometers per second.

29
00:01:38,463 --> 00:01:41,277
Over two hundred thousand kilometers per hour.

30
00:01:41,277 --> 00:01:43,664
Sixty times faster than a rifle bullet.

31
00:01:44,363 --> 00:01:44,980
Floor two.

32
00:01:44,980 --> 00:01:45,597
The entry.

33
00:01:45,597 --> 00:01:49,299
We know exactly what this feels like, because we've done it.

34
00:01:49,299 --> 00:01:56,886
In December nineteen ninety five, NASA's Galileo probe hit Jupiter's atmosphere at about forty seven kilometers per second.

35
00:01:57,586 --> 00:02:04,964
Within about a minute, the air slowed it down so violently that the probe felt two hundred and twenty eight times its own weight.

36
00:02:04,964 --> 00:02:10,970
The front of its heat shield reached around sixteen thousand degrees, hotter than the surface of the Sun.

37
00:02:10,970 --> 00:02:13,543
A person would not survive that first minute.

38
00:02:14,243 --> 00:02:18,029
So from here on, imagine you're inside an unbreakable capsule.

39
00:02:18,029 --> 00:02:20,349
It can't burn and it can't be crushed.

40
00:02:20,349 --> 00:02:22,425
It can only fall, and report back.

41
00:02:22,425 --> 00:02:25,722
And even after the braking, there's Jupiter's gravity.

42
00:02:25,722 --> 00:02:27,492
Two and a half times Earth's.

43
00:02:27,492 --> 00:02:32,377
If you weigh seventy kilos at home, you'd feel like a hundred and seventy seven.

44
00:02:33,077 --> 00:02:33,822
Floor three.

45
00:02:33,822 --> 00:02:34,505
The clouds.

46
00:02:34,505 --> 00:02:39,102
The colorful stripes you know from photos are only the top of the weather.

47
00:02:39,102 --> 00:02:43,326
Models say there are three cloud decks stacked on top of each other.

48
00:02:43,326 --> 00:02:46,432
First, white clouds of ammonia ice, freezing cold.

49
00:02:47,131 --> 00:02:54,625
Below them, a brownish deck of ammonium hydrosulfide, a compound of ammonia and sulfur that smells like rotten eggs.

50
00:02:54,625 --> 00:02:58,566
And deeper still, clouds of water, flickering with lightning.

51
00:02:59,266 --> 00:03:00,217
And it's windy.

52
00:03:00,217 --> 00:03:06,554
As Galileo sank under its parachute, it measured the winds getting stronger, not weaker, with depth.

53
00:03:06,554 --> 00:03:12,130
Over six hundred kilometers per hour, far below the clouds where sunlight never reaches.

54
00:03:12,830 --> 00:03:13,493
Floor four.

55
00:03:13,493 --> 00:03:16,024
About a hundred and fifty kilometers down.

56
00:03:16,024 --> 00:03:18,435
This is where our real explorer stopped.

57
00:03:18,435 --> 00:03:22,231
After fifty eight minutes of sending data, Galileo went silent.

58
00:03:22,231 --> 00:03:28,318
The pressure was about twenty three times Earth's air, and it was around a hundred and fifty degrees.

59
00:03:29,018 --> 00:03:33,730
That sounds tough, but it's like being two hundred and twenty meters under the sea.

60
00:03:33,730 --> 00:03:35,490
Submarines go deeper than that.

61
00:03:35,490 --> 00:03:37,250
We've barely scratched Jupiter.

62
00:03:37,250 --> 00:03:42,019
Galileo made it through less than a quarter of one percent of the way to the center.

63
00:03:42,019 --> 00:03:46,106
At its average pace, the full trip down would take around nineteen days.

64
00:03:46,806 --> 00:03:47,474
Floor five.

65
00:03:47,474 --> 00:03:51,056
You'd expect to splash into an ocean somewhere around here.

66
00:03:51,056 --> 00:03:52,089
But you never do.

67
00:03:52,089 --> 00:04:02,107
Hydrogen, the main ingredient of Jupiter, stops behaving like a normal gas or liquid above about minus two hundred and forty degrees, once it's squeezed hard enough.

68
00:04:02,107 --> 00:04:04,961
Here it's hundreds of degrees hotter than that.

69
00:04:05,661 --> 00:04:09,142
So the air simply gets thicker, and thicker, and thicker.

70
00:04:09,142 --> 00:04:13,538
It turns into something like a hot, dark syrup, with no line in between.

71
00:04:13,538 --> 00:04:16,958
There's no moment where the sky ends and the sea begins.

72
00:04:16,958 --> 00:04:22,087
Your capsule just keeps sinking, slower and slower, through a fluid that never ends.

73
00:04:22,787 --> 00:04:25,223
Even Jupiter's famous storm goes deep.

74
00:04:25,223 --> 00:04:33,873
In twenty twenty one, NASA's Juno spacecraft found that the roots of the Great Red Spot reach up to about five hundred kilometers down.

75
00:04:33,873 --> 00:04:39,897
A storm wider than Earth, and taller than the entire layer of air we live in, many times over.

76
00:04:40,597 --> 00:04:42,397
Now we go much, much deeper.

77
00:04:42,397 --> 00:04:45,355
Floor six, about ten thousand kilometers down.

78
00:04:45,355 --> 00:04:48,120
Almost the width of Earth below the clouds.

79
00:04:48,120 --> 00:04:54,357
The pressure here is one to two million times Earth's air, and it's around five thousand degrees.

80
00:04:55,057 --> 00:04:57,900
And something incredible happens to hydrogen.

81
00:04:57,900 --> 00:05:04,030
It's squeezed so hard that its electrons break free, and it starts to behave like a liquid metal.

82
00:05:04,030 --> 00:05:05,547
It conducts electricity.

83
00:05:05,547 --> 00:05:11,739
Churning around, this ocean of metal powers Jupiter's magnetic field, the strongest of any planet.

84
00:05:12,439 --> 00:05:16,558
Right at the top of that metal ocean, scientists think it rains.

85
00:05:16,558 --> 00:05:17,201
Not water.

86
00:05:17,201 --> 00:05:17,652
Helium.

87
00:05:17,652 --> 00:05:23,765
Droplets of helium sink through the hydrogen like rain, and they may carry neon down with them.

88
00:05:23,765 --> 00:05:28,205
That would explain why Galileo found so little neon in the air above.

89
00:05:28,905 --> 00:05:29,722
Floor seven.

90
00:05:29,722 --> 00:05:32,785
For decades, the textbook picture was simple.

91
00:05:32,785 --> 00:05:38,367
Deep inside Jupiter, a solid ball of rock and ice, a few times heavier than Earth.

92
00:05:38,367 --> 00:05:43,881
Then Juno started measuring Jupiter's gravity, very precisely, orbit after orbit.

93
00:05:44,581 --> 00:05:46,511
And the answer was a surprise.

94
00:05:46,511 --> 00:05:49,470
The heavy stuff isn't packed into a neat ball.

95
00:05:49,470 --> 00:05:54,875
It's smeared out, mixed with hydrogen, spread across maybe half of Jupiter's radius.

96
00:05:54,875 --> 00:05:56,998
Scientists call it a dilute core.

97
00:05:56,998 --> 00:06:00,858
One idea is that a giant collision, long ago, stirred it up.

98
00:06:01,558 --> 00:06:03,571
How much heavy stuff is in there?

99
00:06:03,571 --> 00:06:08,754
Estimates range from about seven to twenty five Earths' worth of rock, ice and metal.

100
00:06:08,754 --> 00:06:13,633
It's like dissolving a few whole planets into a giant ball of gas, and stirring.

101
00:06:14,333 --> 00:06:15,047
Floor eight.

102
00:06:15,047 --> 00:06:15,702
The center.

103
00:06:15,702 --> 00:06:20,227
Seventy thousand kilometers down, five and a half Earths stacked end to end.

104
00:06:20,227 --> 00:06:27,133
Models put the temperature around twenty thousand degrees, more than three times hotter than the surface of the Sun.

105
00:06:27,133 --> 00:06:28,145
And the pressure?

106
00:06:28,145 --> 00:06:31,122
Tens of millions of times what you feel right now.

107
00:06:31,822 --> 00:06:34,361
And even here, there may be no solid floor.

108
00:06:34,361 --> 00:06:39,910
You started falling at the top of the air, and you'd end in the middle of a hot, crushed soup.

109
00:06:39,910 --> 00:06:42,154
In a sense, you never stopped falling.

110
00:06:42,154 --> 00:06:43,747
You just ran out of planet.

111
00:06:44,447 --> 00:06:45,547
Here's the twist.

112
00:06:45,547 --> 00:06:49,102
Things really have fallen into Jupiter, and we watched.

113
00:06:49,102 --> 00:06:59,253
In July nineteen ninety four, the comet Shoemaker Levy nine, broken into about twenty one pieces, slammed into Jupiter one piece after another over six days.

114
00:06:59,953 --> 00:07:06,358
Each piece hit at about sixty kilometers per second, almost exactly the speed you'd arrive at.

115
00:07:06,358 --> 00:07:13,990
The biggest one, fragment G, released energy estimated at around six million megatons of TNT, in a single flash.

116
00:07:14,690 --> 00:07:19,253
The impacts left dark scars in the clouds, some of them bigger than Earth.

117
00:07:19,253 --> 00:07:21,411
Backyard telescopes could see them.

118
00:07:21,411 --> 00:07:25,911
A comet a few kilometers wide, and it bruised the biggest planet we have.

119
00:07:26,611 --> 00:07:29,491
And in twenty oh three, NASA did it on purpose.

120
00:07:29,491 --> 00:07:40,828
The Galileo orbiter, low on fuel, was steered into Jupiter at about forty eight kilometers per second, so it could never crash into Europa and contaminate an ocean that might hold life.

121
00:07:40,828 --> 00:07:43,891
Somewhere down there, its atoms are still falling.

122
00:07:44,591 --> 00:07:46,626
So, our fall, floor by floor.

123
00:07:46,626 --> 00:07:49,714
Fifty nine kilometers per second at the top.

124
00:07:49,714 --> 00:07:52,591
Clouds of ammonia, rotten eggs and water.

125
00:07:52,591 --> 00:07:56,521
Galileo's last signal at a hundred and fifty kilometers.

126
00:07:56,521 --> 00:07:58,275
An ocean with no surface.

127
00:07:58,275 --> 00:07:59,047
Metal rain.

128
00:07:59,047 --> 00:07:59,959
A fuzzy core.

129
00:07:59,959 --> 00:08:01,573
And no ground anywhere.

130
00:08:02,273 --> 00:08:05,651
Next time, we stay closer to home and give Earth a gift.

131
00:08:05,651 --> 00:08:07,401
What if we had a second Moon?

132
00:08:07,401 --> 00:08:10,115
Tides, nights, and maybe a crash, all change.

133
00:08:10,115 --> 00:08:12,951
Subscribe to Cosmic Scale so you don't miss it.

134
00:08:12,951 --> 00:08:16,993
And tell me in the comments, which planet should we fall into next?
